---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="kicker">A programming language for AI and data work</p>
    <h1>Describe the experiment.<br /><em>Watch it learn.</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Milo is a language for building, training, and fine-tuning models. It
      reads close to plain English where that is clear, and stays a fast,
      statically analysable language underneath — with tensors, datasets, and
      training loops as first-class features, not library afterthoughts.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="/guides/getting-started/">Get started</a>
      <a class="button" href="/docs/spec/language-spec-v0.1/">Read the specification</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="features">
    <article class="feature">
      <span class="tag">Plain English</span>
      <h3>Sentences that mean one thing</h3>
      <p>
        A sentence is a fixed template, not free-form prose. That is what
        keeps the language unambiguous — and what lets a compiler check it.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <span class="tag">Tensors</span>
      <h3>The tensor type is built in</h3>
      <p>
        Shapes, dtypes, broadcasting, and matrix products, with AVX2 and
        NEON kernels chosen at run time. Copying a tensor copies the
        handle, not the data.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <span class="tag">Differentiation</span>
      <h3>Gradients you can check</h3>
      <p>
        Reverse-mode autograd, with every adjoint rule tested against finite
        differences. A training loop is a loop.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <span class="tag">Models</span>
      <h3>Safetensors, natively</h3>
      <p>
        Memory-mapped weights: opening a seventy-gigabyte checkpoint costs
        nothing, and only the tensors you use become resident.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <span class="tag">Small cards</span>
      <h3>Know what will fit</h3>
      <p>
        Quantisation, low-rank updates, and a planner that tells you what a
        run will need — and what you could change. It never changes
        anything itself.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <span class="tag">Interoperable</span>
      <h3>A stable C ABI</h3>
      <p>
        The runtime is plain C at the boundary. Other languages, and the
        package manager, link against that and nothing else.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 1rem">
    <pre><code>{`load model "llama-3-8b.safetensors" as base
load data "support_chats.csv" as chats

split chats into train and test by 90 percent

fine-tune base on train
    using lora with rank 16
    precision is bf16
    batch size 4
    epochs 3
    learning rate 0.0002
    save to "my-model.safetensors"

evaluate base on test and show accuracy`}</code></pre>
  </section>
</Base>
